    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d136">

                <pb facs="0502" n="461" sameAs="frus:frus1981-88v04/pg461"/>

                <head>136. Memorandum From the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                    (McFarlane) to President Reagan<anchor n="1" xml:id="d136-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1983-11-16">November 16, 1983</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Discussion of Channels to the Soviets</item>
                </list>
                <p>Secretary Shultz has advised me that he intends to discuss alternative approaches
                    for dealing with the Soviets during his meeting with you today at 1:30.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d136-f2"/></p>
                <p>This is an extremely complex, important and timely issue. Numerous analysts and
                    observers returning from the Soviet Union in recent weeks have reported
                    uniformly a high level of anxiety among Soviet <pb facs="0503" n="462"
                        xml:id="pg462"/> leaders, and apparently sincere interest in communication,
                    but a frustration at not knowing how to make it happen.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d136-f3"/></p>
                <p>You have persistently tried to arrange such an authoritative discreet channel.
                    For various reasons the Soviets have not responded. Having observed
                        successful<anchor n="4" xml:id="d136-f4"/> efforts by three Administrations,
                    I believe I may have something useful to offer both on the substance and
                    mechanics of doing business with the Russians. Subject to your approval I would
                    like to attend your session today with George.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d136-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d136-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Reagan Library, Robert McFarlane Files, Chronological File, Chron
                    (Official) November 1983; NLR–362–6–10–5–7. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d136-f2" n="2" xml:id="d136-f2-content">
                <p>According to the President’s Daily Diary, Reagan held a private afternoon meeting
                    with Shultz on November 16 before an NSPG meeting. (Reagan Library, President’s
                    Daily Diary) Reagan wrote in his diary: “Met with Geo. S. about establishing a
                    pipe line outside the bureaucracy for direct contact with Soviets.” (Brinkley,
                    ed., <hi rendition="#italic">The Reagan Diaries</hi>, vol. I, January
                    1981–October 1985, p. 198)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d136-f3" n="3" xml:id="d136-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d132">Document 132</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d136-f4" n="4" xml:id="d136-f4-content">
                <p>McFarlane inserted “and unsuccessful” in the margin.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
